---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/results.json';

const MAC = 'mac-m4-pro-24g';
const PC = 'win-rtx3060-12g';
const hw = data.hardware[MAC];
const k = (c) => (c == null ? '–' : `${Math.round(c / 1024)}k`);
const fmt = (a) => (a ? `${a[0]}/${a[1]}` : '–');
const rows = data.models
  .filter((m) => m.perf[MAC])
  .map((m) => {
    const p = m.perf[MAC];
    const pcV = m.perf[PC]?.viable ?? m.viable;
    const fail = p.viable?.ladder?.find((x) => !x.viable);
    return {
      m, mlx: m.format === 'mlx',
      tps16: p.sweep?.['16384']?.tps ?? p.tps, tps64: p.sweep?.['65536']?.tps,
      jpt: p.sweep?.['16384']?.j_per_token,
      ctx: p.viable?.viable_ctx, pcCtx: m.format === 'mlx' ? null : pcV?.viable_ctx,
      fail: fail ? `${k(fail.ctx)}: ${fail.fail_reason}` : null,
    };
  })
  .sort((a, b) => (a.m.id.split(':')[0] + a.mlx).localeCompare(b.m.id.split(':')[0] + b.mlx));
---
<Base title="Apple silicon" description="What runs well on a 24 GB MacBook Pro: viable context, speed, GGUF versus MLX, and what it feels like to use." prose>
  <h1>What runs well on a 24 GB MacBook Pro</h1>
  <p>
    {hw.label}, {hw.bandwidth_gbs} GB/s memory bandwidth, {hw.os}. The same tests as the PC, with the same model files where possible,
    plus MLX builds of three of them. Quality scores travel with the model file; speed, context and the experience belong to the machine.
  </p>

  <h2>The short answer</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Models up to about 14 GB run well</b> and leave the Mac usable: Gemma 4 12B, Qwen 3.5 9B and gpt-oss 20B.</li>
    <li><b>Usable context is about half the PC's.</b> 32k for Gemma and Qwen, 64k for gpt-oss, against 64k to 128k on the PC. Every failure was speed, not recall.</li>
    <li><b>MLX is faster for Qwen and gpt-oss</b> (about 30 percent), the same for Gemma, and a little less careful with facts unless told to say when it is unsure.</li>
    <li><b>A 20 GB model fits only by force</b>, and then the whole Mac slows down. The PC runs the same model and stays usable.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Every model measured on the Mac</h2>
  <div class="wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Format</th><th>tok/s at 16k</th><th>tok/s at 64k</th><th>Viable context (Mac)</th><th>Viable context (PC)</th><th>First failure</th><th>Code</th><th>Tools</th><th>Fake items caught</th><th>J per token (GPU)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {rows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td><a href={`/models/${r.m.slug}/`}>{r.m.id}</a></td><td>{r.mlx ? 'MLX' : 'GGUF'}</td>
            <td>{r.tps16 ?? '–'}</td><td>{r.tps64 ?? '–'}</td><td><b>{k(r.ctx)}</b></td><td>{r.mlx ? 'n/a' : k(r.pcCtx)}</td>
            <td>{r.fail ?? 'none'}</td><td>{fmt(r.m.quality.code)}</td><td>{fmt(r.m.quality.tools)}</td><td>{fmt(r.m.quality.fake_caught)}</td>
            <td>{r.jpt ?? 'not measured'}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="note">
    GGUF rows use the same files as the PC, so their quality columns are the PC's scores. MLX rows are different files and were scored on the Mac.
    Viable context is the largest size where the model recalled all planted facts at 15 tok/s or more, within 180 seconds. Laguna was tested only up to 32k.
  </p>

  <h2>What it is like to use</h2>
  <p>
    The PC's graphics card has its own 12 GB, and the rest of the machine has 128 GB to itself. When a model is too big, it spills into system
    memory and only the model slows down. On the Mac, the GPU, macOS and every app share the same 24 GB. That makes small models fast, because nothing
    is copied between memories, but there is no reserve: whatever the model takes, the rest of the Mac does without.
  </p>
  <p>
    Laguna XS 2.1 (20 GB) shows the difference. It does not load until the GPU memory limit is raised from about 16 GB to 20 GB
    (<code>sudo sysctl iogpu.wired_limit_mb=20480</code>, reset at reboot). It then passed at 8k and 16k, and failed at 32k at 5 tok/s.
    It still recalled every planted fact; it was simply too slow. While it ran, swap filled and the whole Mac lagged. On the PC the same model
    reached 32k and the machine stayed in normal daily use throughout.
  </p>

  <h2>Which model for which job on 24 GB</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Long documents and agent work: gpt-oss 20B.</b> The fastest here, viable to 64k, the cheapest per token. Weak at spotting invented facts unless the system prompt asks it to say when it is unsure.</li>
    <li><b>General work and careful answers: Qwen 3.5 9B.</b> Small enough to leave plenty of room for other apps. The GGUF build caught all three invented facts unprompted; the faster MLX build caught one, but passed every tool task.</li>
    <li><b>Gemma 4 12B</b> is the slowest of the three here and gains nothing from MLX, whose build also looped on two tool tasks. Prefer the GGUF build.</li>
    <li><b>Anything over about 14 GB:</b> not on this machine if you also want to work on it. Use a bigger Mac, a PC with a separate GPU, or split the job into smaller steps.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it was measured</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Runtimes: {hw.runtime} for GGUF files, and mlx-lm through a small Ollama-compatible bridge for MLX, so every test ran unchanged.</li>
    <li>Same prompts, temperature 0, fixed seed, thinking off, the same 16k quality context and 16k to 64k speed runs as the PC.</li>
    <li>GPU power from macOS <code>powermetrics</code>, sampled every half second. It covers the GPU part of the chip only, like the PC figure, which covers the graphics card only. MLX power was not measured.</li>
    <li>Every record carries the machine, runtime and file format, so Mac and PC numbers are never mixed in one comparison. See <a href="/hardware/">Hardware</a>.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
